#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055965 :tutorial/content "Let's remind ourselves of the first few powers of $\\textrm{i}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\textrm{i}^{1} &= \\textrm{i} \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{2} &= -1 \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{3} &= -\\textrm{i} \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{4} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the fourth power, we return to the value of $1$, which can also be written as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^0 = 1\n$$\n\nThis means that if we continue to increase the powers, we would cycle through the same four values once again, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3f3a42dd591daf109e474eec99e446706e0d8e46f1252540839cd50d957463.png)\n\nSo, for $\\textrm{i}^5$ through $\\textrm{i}^8$, the exact same pattern repeats. Remembering that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^4 = 1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\textrm{i}^5&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i} &= \\textrm{i} \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^6&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^2 &=-1 \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^7&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^3 &= -\\textrm{i} \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^8&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^4 &= 1. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for $\\textrm{i}^9$ through to $\\textrm{i}^{12}$, the pattern repeats again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\textrm{i}^9&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i} &= \\textrm{i} \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^{10}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^2 &=-1 \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^{11}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^3 &= -\\textrm{i} \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^{12}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^4 &= 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to simplify a higher integer power of $\\textrm{i}$, we *divide the power by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder*.\n\nLet's see this idea in action." :tutorial/id #uuid "be735724-c1e7-5d1c-b396-61d58ee81903" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2762 :tutorial/title "The Cyclic Nature of Powers of i"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055966 :tutorial/content "Let's compute the first few powers of $\\textrm{i}$, where\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i} = \\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\textrm{i}^{0} &= 1 \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{1} &= \\textrm{i} \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{2} &= -1 \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{3} &= \\textrm{i}^{2} \\cdot \\textrm{i} = (-1) \\cdot \\textrm{i} =-\\textrm{i} \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{4} &= \\textrm{i}^{2} \\cdot \\textrm{i}^{2} = (-1) \\cdot (-1) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use these results to evaluate expressions containing powers of $\\textrm{i}$. Let's take a look at some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "6fe2451a-f8be-5178-a84f-66817536929d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2763 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
